//? Title: B-spline regression
//? Subtitle: A reproducible cubic spline fit in C++, Python and R
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

Choose a language tab for each example. Code and its results stay together.
Run each tab independently. Python uses the local package in
`build/venv/pydatamunge-run`; R uses `build/r/library`. Build these once with
`just build-python` and `just build-r`, and rebuild after binding changes.

> B-spline regression

Fit a cubic B-spline basis to noisy observations. This is least-squares
regression: the fitted curve need not pass through each observation.
`bs(lower, upper, knots)` returns an owned `Bspline` for training and prediction;
its default omits the first basis column so `LM` can add an intercept.
The 100 breakpoints include both endpoints and produce 101 predictor columns.
`speval()` creates the design directly in CSR storage. `lm(y, X)` returns an
owned `LM` and adds an
intercept by default and chooses dense QR or sparse PCG from the matrix storage;
matrix prediction uses the same predictor order and adds the intercept itself.
The Doppler function accepts the whole vector: its arithmetic, sine and noise
addition form a lazy expression. The forwarding parameter keeps named vectors
borrowed and temporary vectors owned. `rnorm()` draws once when the expression
is formed; model fitting and plotting evaluate using those same noise values.
Use `y.eval()` or `dm::Vector<> y = ...` to keep a computed snapshot. `apply()`
remains an eager mapping operation for arbitrary scalar callbacks.
Sparse fitting retains compressed designs, while inference uses dense
coefficient-sized covariance matrices. The sparse solver uses normal equations,
which square the design's condition number.
With so many unpenalised coefficients, the curve can follow noise as well as
signal. `dm::plot()` starts an empty plot, and `.xlim()` / `.ylim()` set the
axis ranges while retaining fluent chaining. The block selects the C++ Nix
shell through an explicit local Git reference so mep uses matching installed
headers and library. Line colors accept literals or `std::string` hex RGBA text;
the final byte is opacity divided by 255.

All three tabs seed the native generator with 42 and use the same 100
breakpoints. C++ demonstrates an explicit sparse design and `LM`; Python and R
use the bound `BSplineRegression` convenience API for the same least-squares
spline space. Each tab saves its own plot.

\tabs(
\tab(C++,

```{cpp, nix-shell="git+file:../../../../..#cpp", cmd="c++ -std=c++23 -O2 {file} -o {bin} $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge) && {bin}", file="figures/bspline_fit.svg"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  dm::set_seed(42);
  const auto n = 1000, nx = 1000, ks = 100;
  const auto doppler = [](auto&& x) {
    return dm::sin(20 / (std::forward<decltype(x)>(x) + 0.25));
  };
  const auto x = dm::runif(0, 1, n);
  const auto y = doppler(x) + dm::rnorm(0, 1, n);
  const auto k = dm::seql(0, 1, ks);
  const auto basis = dm::bs(0, 1, k);
  const auto xmat = basis.speval(x);
  const auto lm1 = dm::lm(y, xmat);
  const auto seqx = dm::seql(0, 1, nx);
  const auto yhat = lm1.predict(basis.speval(seqx));
  const auto plt = dm::plot()
    .title("Cubic B-spline regression")
    .subtitle("1,000 observations; 100 breakpoints; unpenalised least squares")
    .points(x, y, "Sampled Data", "#2233ddaa")
    .lines(seqx, yhat, "B-spline Fits", "#ff66aa", 5.)
    .ylim(-4., 4.).xlim(0., 1.)
    .x_label("x").y_label("y")
    .size(1000, 600);
  dm::mkdir("build/figs");
  plt.save("build/figs/bspline_fit.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/bspline_fit.svg)
// result_end

)
\tab(Python,

`BSplineRegression` fits the same cubic spline space directly from vectors
and breakpoints. Plotting also uses the native library.

```{python, nix-shell="git+file:../../../../..#python", cmd="env -u PYTHONPATH ../../../../../build/venv/pydatamunge-run/bin/python {file}", file="figures/bspline_fit_python.svg"}
import math
from pathlib import Path
from pydatamunge import datamunge as dm

dm.set_seed(42)
x = list(dm.runif(0, 1, 1000))
noise = list(dm.rnorm(0, 1, 1000))
y = [math.sin(20 / (value + 0.25)) + error for value, error in zip(x, noise)]
knots = dm.seql(0, 1, 100)
model = dm.BSplineRegression(x, y, knots, 3)
seqx = list(dm.seql(0, 1, 1000))
yhat = list(model.predict(seqx))
plot = dm.RPlot.create()
plot.points(x, y, "Sampled Data", dm.RGB("#2233ddaa"))
plot.lines(seqx, yhat, "B-spline Fits", dm.RGB("#ff66aa"), 5.0)
plot.set_title("Cubic B-spline regression")
plot.set_subtitle("1,000 observations; 100 breakpoints; unpenalised least squares")
plot.xlim(0, 1)
plot.ylim(-4, 4)
plot.set_xlab("x")
plot.set_ylab("y")
plot.size(1000, 600)
Path("build/figs").mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
plot.save_svg("build/figs/bspline_fit_python.svg")
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/bspline_fit_python.svg)
// result_end

)
\tab(R,

`BSplineRegression()` fits the same spline space. `native_rnorm(mean, sd, n)`
uses the shared C++ generator; `stats::rnorm(n, mean, sd)` remains available.

```{r, nix-shell="git+file:../../../../..#r", cmd="env R_LIBS_USER=../../../../../build/r/library Rscript {file}", file="figures/bspline_fit_r.svg"}
suppressPackageStartupMessages(library(datamunger))

invisible(set_seed(42L))
x <- as.numeric(unif(0, 1, 1000L))
noise <- as.numeric(native_rnorm(0, 1, 1000L))
y <- sin(20 / (x + 0.25)) + noise
knots <- as.numeric(seql(0, 1, 100L))
model <- BSplineRegression(x, y, knots, 3L)
seqx <- as.numeric(seql(0, 1, 1000L))
yhat <- as.numeric(model$predict(seqx))
plot <- RPlot_create()
invisible(plot$points(x, y, "Sampled Data", RGB("#2233ddaa")))
invisible(plot$lines(seqx, yhat, "B-spline Fits", RGB("#ff66aa"), 5))
invisible(plot$set_title("Cubic B-spline regression"))
invisible(plot$set_subtitle("1,000 observations; 100 breakpoints; unpenalised least squares"))
invisible(plot$xlim(0, 1))
invisible(plot$ylim(-4, 4))
invisible(plot$set_xlab("x"))
invisible(plot$set_ylab("y"))
invisible(plot$size(1000L, 600L))
dir.create("build/figs", recursive = TRUE, showWarnings = FALSE)
invisible(plot$save_svg("build/figs/bspline_fit_r.svg"))
```
